# Summary of changes for run 67c4125f-e2d7-45a5-9d39-c3b226024abd
I wrote the five publication files for the per-N quadratic-sieve yield dial and committed them. No Lean build was run: this was packaging only, and no Lean sources were changed. The LaTeX file was not compiled because `pdflatex` isn't available here; I only checked that its braces and environments are balanced. The plotting scripts also weren't rendered, since `matplotlib` isn't installed. Their calculations do run against a stand-in plotting module.

- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,550 words): a popular-science telling of the dial \(\text{rate}(N)\approx-0.0035+0.01156\cdot\mathrm{QR}(N)\). It states each result inline, in prose:
  - the number of roots of \(x^2\equiv N \pmod p\) is exactly \(1+(N/p)\);
  - Euler's test is exactly the condition \((N/p)=1\);
  - the shape theorem: root mass \(=2\,\mathrm{QR}+\#\{p\mid N\}\);
  - the half-pass law and the population mean in \([8,12)\), giving a mean dial in \([0.08898,0.13522)\);
  - the transfer law \(R^2_{\rm tr}(b)=\rho^2-(b-\hat\beta)^2S_{xx}/S_{yy}\);
  - the impossibility of the reported pair 0.2719 / 0.2717 coming from one sample;
  - the negative-dial example \(163520117=2027\cdot80671\);
  - the pure-error floor that bounds any predictor built from this one count.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 4,300 words) and **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`** (`amsart`, mirrors the Markdown paper): abstract, definitions, every theorem stated in full with a proof sketch, three algorithms, applications, limitations and future work. The experimental figures are presented as reported measurements, not as proved results.
- **`demo.py`**: standard library only, runs in about 2 seconds and all checks pass. It tests the root-count law on 7,200 \((N,p)\) pairs, checks periodicity and the weighted yield, and computes the exact population mean (≈ 11.3486, mean dial ≈ 0.1277). It also confirms the negative-dial example and illustrates the transfer law and pure-error floor. Those last two use synthetic data, labelled as such.
- **`PACKAGE.json`**: every schema field is filled in, with domain Cryptography and date 2026-10-09. It contains:
  - 1 demo and 3 algorithms (each with a title, description, pseudocode and type-hinted Python);
  - 3 plotting scripts;
  - 2 interactive HTML widgets: an explorer showing Legendre symbols and roots for any \(N\), and a slider showing the transfer-law curve with the intercept refitted or not;
  - a guided-tour page with collapsible proofs and links;
  - the full source of the 4 Lean files in `lean_proofs`, listed in `lean_files`.

The future-directions text supplied to me was cut off partway through its third item ("Clipped Di…"). I finished that item in a few sentences, drawing only on the negative-dial example and the floor results; the first two items are verbatim. None of the prose files mention Lean, formalization, file paths or code names.
